---
/**
 * Portfolio four: the value lean plus one fund that adds a trend-following program on
 * top of its stocks. The largest bet on the site and the least settled, so the page
 * carries the 96-year result, its decay, the fund's youth and the ladder together.
 */
import AllocationBar from "~/components/AllocationBar.astro";
import Compare from "~/components/Compare.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import HoldingsTable from "~/components/HoldingsTable.astro";
import KeyNumbers from "~/components/KeyNumbers.astro";
import Ladder from "~/components/Ladder.astro";
import { portfolio } from "~/content/portfolios";
import { figureValue } from "~/lib/figures";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

const p = portfolio("with-trend");

const NUMBERS = [
  {
    value: `${await figureValue("trend-96-year-gap")} points`,
    label: "a year over an all-stock fund, 1929 to 2025",
    note: "Range 1.3 to 2.7. Built from a long trend-following series, because the fund is only three years old.",
  },
  {
    value: await figureValue("trend-gap-since-2009"),
    label: "the same gap since 2009",
    note: "Too small to tell from luck",
  },
  {
    value: await figureValue("with-trend-worst-fall"),
    label: "Worst fall, 1990 to 2026",
    note: "With RSST at 25%. Forty-two months to recover.",
  },
  { value: "Sept 2023", label: "RSST launched", note: "Under three years of record" },
];

const HOLDINGS = [
  {
    ticker: "RSST",
    what: "Return Stacked U.S. Stocks & Managed Futures ETF. For every dollar, about a dollar of large US stocks and about a dollar of a trend-following futures program, borrowed inside the fund.",
    weight: "30%",
    fee: "0.99%",
  },
  { ticker: "VTI", what: "The whole US market", weight: "19%", fee: "0.03%" },
  { ticker: "VXUS", what: "Every market outside the US", weight: "16%", fee: "0.05%" },
  { ticker: "VTV", what: "Large, cheap US companies", weight: "15%", fee: "0.03%" },
  { ticker: "AVDV", what: "Small, cheap, profitable companies in developed markets outside the US", weight: "10%", fee: "0.36%" },
  { ticker: "IDMO", what: "Developed-market companies outside the US whose shares have been rising", weight: "5%", fee: "0.25%" },
  { ticker: "AVES", what: "Cheap companies in emerging markets", weight: "5%", fee: "0.36%" },
];

const LADDER = [
  { worstFall: "−30%", rsst: "11%" },
  { worstFall: "−40%", rsst: "15%", extra: "Plus 10 points in inflation-protected Treasuries (TIPS)" },
  { worstFall: "−50%", rsst: "19%", extra: "Plus 10 points in TIPS" },
  { worstFall: "−60%", rsst: "24%" },
  { worstFall: "About −70%", rsst: "30%", published: true },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="The same, plus trend"
  description="The value lean plus one fund that adds a trend-following program on top of its stocks. What that is, why it can help a whole portfolio, what 96 years of data say, and how much of it to hold."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="Portfolio 4 of 4"
    title="The same, plus trend"
    lede="Portfolio three, with 30% moved into a fund that holds US stocks and a trend-following program at the same time. The one holding on this site that adds an idea on top of stocks instead of swapping stocks for it."
  >
    <KeyNumbers items={NUMBERS} />
  </Hero>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2>What you hold</h2>
  </div>

  <HoldingsTable rows={HOLDINGS} class="my-4" />
  <AllocationBar holdings={p.holdings} name={p.name} class="my-6" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The fee is 0.38% a year, or $38 on $10,000, and almost all of it is RSST's 0.99%. Because RSST holds a dollar
    of stocks for every dollar you put in, the portfolio still owns about as much US stock as portfolio three; the
    30% buys a trend-following program of the same size on top. There are no bonds. The ladder below is where they
    come back in.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What trend following is</h2>

    <p>A trend-following program is a rule, not a manager's opinion. It trades futures, contracts that bet on
    the price of a stock index, a government bond, a currency or a commodity without owning it. It buys what has
    been rising over the past several months and sells short, that is, bets against, what has been falling. In a
    long, grinding fall it ends up betting against the things that are falling: in 2022, by the SG Trend index's
    own count, the strategy made 27% while the S&amp;P 500 lost 18%. In a sudden crash like March 2020 it has no
    time to switch and does not help.</p>

    <p>Since 2000, trend following rose in 64% of the worst months for stocks, more than gold at 59%, Treasuries at
    55% to 58%, or anything else I measured. It will not save you in a crash, but it tends to do well in the
    years stocks do worst, and nothing else in this portfolio does that.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Why on top, and not instead</h2>

    <p>Most managed-futures funds ask you to sell stocks to buy them. Stocks earn more than trend does over the long
    run, so every dollar moved out of stocks gives up return to buy the diversification. RSST borrows inside the
    fund to hold the stocks and the trend program at once. Sell $30 of every $100 in stocks to buy trend and you
    own $70 of stocks. Hold RSST at 30% and you still own about $100 of stocks plus $30 of trend. Over 1990 to 2026, buying the same amount of trend by
    selling stocks instead of adding it on top would have cost about
    <Figure id="financing-vs-selling" inline showUnit={false} /> a year of growth. That is why a strategy that
    looks ordinary on its own can help a portfolio when it is stacked on top: you keep the stock return and add
    the trend return, rather than averaging the two.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What the record says</h2>

    <p>I do not have a century of RSST, so the long test uses a trend-following series built from the same rule and
    scaled to the size of the fund's program. Over the 96 years from 1929 to 2025 a portfolio of 70% US stocks
    with a 30% RSST-style holding earned about <Figure id="trend-96-year-gap" inline /> above a cheap
    all-stock fund, with a range of 1.3 to 2.7. By decade the gap ran between 0.5 and 3.6 points
    through the 2000s, then 0.6 in the 2010s and 0.3 in the 2020s so far. Since 2009 the gap is
    <Figure id="trend-gap-since-2009" inline showUnit={false} /> a year, too small to tell from luck. Trend
    following has done less since 2008 than before it on every version of the rule I checked, and nobody knows
    whether that continues.</p>

    <p>On the shorter 1990 to 2026 window, using the actual funds' behaviour, the portfolio with RSST at 25%
    fell <Figure id="with-trend-worst-fall" inline showUnit={false} /> at worst and took 42 months to recover,
    against 52.7% and 63 months for the cheap mix. At the more modest returns I would expect from here, that 25%
    version's chance of trailing a cheap index fund is about one in four over ten years, one in six over twenty
    and one in nine over thirty (<Figure id="trailing-odds" inline />).</p>

    <p>The decade that makes the case is 1999 to 2009. US stocks returned <Figure id="flat-decade" inline /> for
    ten years. A 30% trend holding on top would have turned that into about 0.05% a year. The protection on offer
    is against a lost decade, not against a crash.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What is not known</h2>

    <p>RSST launched in September 2023. Its own record settles nothing either way, because the fund exists for the
    years stocks lose, and there have not been any yet. How closely its program tracks the long-run series rests on
    31 months of filings, and no issuer discloses the cost of the borrowing inside the fund. Of the managed-futures
    ETFs filing reports in mid-2019, <Figure id="managed-futures-attrition" inline showUnit={false} /> had stopped
    by the end of 2025. If RSST closes, the fallback is <a href="/portfolios/value-lean/">portfolio three</a>, with
    the 30% going back into VTI.</p>

    <p>Funds running the same idea differ far more than index funds do, so I chose RSST over the alternatives on
    its age, size and trading costs, not on return. Its gains are taxed as ordinary income, so hold it in a
    retirement account or do not hold it. The <a href="/tools/which-account/">which-account tool</a> puts it
    first in the queue for shelter at every tax rate, as long as its income is counted the way its filings record
    it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>How much to hold</h2>

    <p>Nearly everything that makes this portfolio differ from the market comes from RSST, so its share is the one
    decision that matters. The share is set by the worst fall you would sit through without selling. The published
    30% assumes a tolerance near 70%, which is more than most people mean when they say they can hold on.</p>
  </div>

  <Ladder rows={LADDER} class="my-6" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The TIPS rows come from today's prices. US stocks are priced at 41 times their ten-year average earnings, a
    level reached only once before, in 1999 and 2000. In the two past episodes when the market was above 30 times,
    the typical worst fall after inflation over the following 15 years was about
    <Figure id="worst-fall-above-cape-30" inline showUnit={false} />, against 37% from cheaper starting points.
    Two episodes are a warning, not a forecast, so I let that concern in only through the fall you plan for, never
    as a prediction of returns. Ten-year TIPS pay 2.44% a year above inflation, the highest since 2008, which makes
    them the natural home for the money that leaves RSST.</p>

    <p><strong>The version for someone worried about today's prices.</strong> Hold RSST at 25% and put 5 points
    into ten-year TIPS, inside a traditional IRA or 401(k). It sits between the −50% and −60% rows. Over the past
    96 years it would have trailed the 30% version by about
    <Figure id="working-default-gap" inline showUnit={false} /> a year. At the returns I would expect from here it
    trails by 0 to 0.2 points, and it comes out ahead only if stocks beat bonds by less than about 1.5 points a
    year. In exchange, its worst fall is 1.8 points shallower. That is the price of betting that today's high prices
    matter.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What losing feels like</h2>

    <p>Losing here looks like a decade of quiet, monthly underperformance against a plain US index fund, not a
    crash. At its worst, trend following ran <Figure id="trend-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} /> the stocks it
    replaced, over 11.2 years. In <Figure id="abandonment-30" inline showUnit={false} /> of simulated thirty-year
    histories, the 30% version fell far enough behind a plain index fund that most people would have given up on it,
    and in <Figure id="abandonment-30-dead" inline showUnit={false} /> if trend following has stopped paying
    altogether.
    Choose 25% if you think you would sell.</p>
  </div>

  <Compare class="my-8">
    <p slot="get">A holding that tends to rise in the years stocks fall, added on top of your stocks rather than in
    place of them. About 2 points a year over an all-stock fund on 96 years of data, and a flat decade instead of
    a lost one in 1999 to 2009.</p>
    <p slot="give">A 0.99% fee on 30% of your money. A fund under three years old, from a category where half the
    funds closed in six years. A gap that has been too small to see since 2009. Years at a time of trailing a
    plain index fund while wondering whether the idea is broken.</p>
  </Compare>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>Figures on this page are before tax and after fund costs, including the borrowing inside the fund. The
    96-year result is built from a trend series, not a fund's record, and every result depends on what trend
    following earns from here, which nobody can measure in advance.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
